    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9b692692-b480-5366-81a2-dfbbb61fb77d",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral $\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{24}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} 6\\csc^{2}(4 x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=4 x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{24}$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{24}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} 6\\csc^{2}(4 x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 6\\csc^{2}(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\csc^{2}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =- \\frac{3}{2}\\cot(u)\\big|_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =- \\frac{3}{2}(\\cot(\\frac{\\pi}{2})-\\cot(\\frac{\\pi}{6})) \\\\ & =- \\frac{3}{2}(0-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
