      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral $\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} 6\\csc^{2}(2 x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=2 x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} 6\\csc^{2}(2 x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 6\\csc^{2}(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =3\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\csc^{2}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =-3\\cot(u)\\big|_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =-3(\\cot(\\frac{\\pi}{2})-\\cot(\\frac{\\pi}{3})) \\\\ & =-3(0-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
